# Importing data
Need to create a frequency spectrum (fs) from my AD files. AD files should be filtered but not thinned. 
EX: fs = dadi.Spectrum([0, 100, 20, 10, 1, 0])

This creates a spectrum object, representing the FS from a SINGLE population. Given the size of my dataset, I want to focus on automatic methods of generating a spectrum object. To do this, I'll load a pre-generated FS from a file using: fs = dadi.Spectrum.from_file(filename) 

# Frequency spectrum file format
Each file begins with anny number of comment lines beginning with #
First non-comment line contain P integers, giving dimensions of the FS array. P is the number of pops represented (2 in this case).
For a FS representing data from 4x4x2 samples (I assume 2 pops, 4 observations/samples/...sites?) will have the dimensions 5x5x3 (Each dimension is one larger than the number of samples, because the number of observations can range, for example, from 0 to 4 if there are 4 samples, for a total of 5 possibilities.)

On the same line, the string 'folded' or 'unfolded' denoting if the FS is folded. See above for difference. 

# Types of Data Files for input
#VCF
The file corresponding to the Spectrum fs can be written using the command: fs.to_file(filename)
Data can be loaded from VCF files using: make_data_dict_vcf in dadi .Misc submodule. This requires the name of the VCF (can by zipped up [*.vcf.ga]). Second half of the file is txt, two column containing the individual names in one and their respective pop in column 2. 

To generate the FS with .vcf files, need to create a data dictionary following: 
dd = dadi.Misc.make_data_dict_vcf("example.vcf.gz", "popfile.txt")
fs = dadi.Spectrum.from_data_dict(dd, ['pop0', 'pop1'], projections = [20, 30], polarized = False)

#SNP
The method Spectrum.from_data_dict can be used to create a Spectrum using: fs = Spectrum.from_data_dict(dd, pop_id = ['YRI', 'CEU'], projections = [10, 12], polarized = True)
pop_ids specifies which populations to use to create the FS and their order
projections denotes the population sample sizes for resulting FS (2 samples for each individual- diploid).
polarized specifies if dadi should use an outgroup to polarize the SNPs. =TRUE snps wit or without outgroup info will be ignored. =FALSE outgroup info will be ignored and resuting spectrum will be folded. 
If data has missing calls for some individuals, projecting down to a smaller sample size will increase the # of snps in the analysis. Downside is some fraction of the snps will project down to a freq of 0 and will be uninformative 
Typically- often choose our projection to maximize the number of segregating sites found in final FS assessed with fs.S(). 

How to generate fs from SNP data:
data file begins with any number of comment lines
first line passed is a column header line. header arbitrary
first column contains the in group reference sequence at that snp, including flanking bases. If bases are unknown: -
second column contains aligned outgroup ref sequece at that snp. header arbitrary
third column gives first segregating allele. Column header must be Allele1
Following is an arbitrary number of columns, one for each pop, giving the number of times Allele1 was observed in that pop. Header for each column should be pop name. 
Next column gives second segragating allele. Header must be Allele2
Follows is the pops and how many times allele2 was observed. Header/column identifier should be in same order as allele1
Followed by arbitrary number of columns which will be concatenated with _ to assign label for each snp
Method Misc.make_data_dict reads the above snp file format to generate a python data dictionary describing the data: dd = Misc.make_data_dict(filename)

